## Q-MULTI. Multi-label label-error detection is a solved shape, but kNN on a shared study embedding is not the published answer

The cleanest published answer is Cleanlab's own multi-label extension:

**Thyagarajan, Snorrason, Northcutt & Mueller, "Identifying Incorrect Annotations in Multi-Label Classification Data" (arXiv:2211.13895, Cleanlab)** — [paper](https://arxiv.org/html/2211.13895).

They extend Confident Learning to multi-label by the one-vs-rest reduction:
- For each of the K binary labels, run CL independently using the K-th predicted probability.
- Flag the union of examples where any binary label is estimated wrong.
- For ranking under a budget, compute per-class self-confidence `s_i^k = b_i^k * p_i^k + (1-b_i^k)*(1-p_i^k)` and pool the K scores into a single example-level quality score.
- The best pooling they find is an **EMA over the scores sorted descending** (alpha=0.8), which emphasises the most-suspect class while still using information from the others. It beats min/mean/median/softmin/log/cumulative-average pooling on synthetic multi-label benchmarks and on CelebA.

**SEMD (Srikanth et al., 2023)** independently uses the same philosophy for multi-label medical images: their "Per-Label Removal" strategy frames the task as K binary problems and runs CL per finding. They test it on CheXpert and METER-ML and find it comparable to an oracle grid-search over per-class removal amounts.

So for our twelve-finding knee-MRI setup, the canonical multi-label CL is **twelve independent CL detectors on the same shared embedding**, with the scores pooled per example. That is option 1 in your letter (per-column independent kNN), and it is exactly what Cleanlab's multi-label code does.

### What that means for SimiFeat-style kNN

SimiFeat assumes one label per sample. Translated to multi-label, the natural extensions are:

1. **Per-column independent kNN** — run kNN label-consistency twelve times on the same embedding. This is the direct analogue of the CL extension. The drawback you identified is real: the neighbour ordering is identical for all twelve detectors, so a globally atypical study is flagged on every column. I did not find a paper that measures how much this degrades precision; the multi-label CL literature treats it as the default and focuses on pooling the per-column scores better.

2. **Label-vector distance** — compute Hamming or Jaccard disagreement between a study's label vector and the label vectors of its k nearest neighbours, then attribute disagreement to individual cells. I found **no published attribution rule** for the last step; it would be ad hoc. The closest conceptual relative is multi-label kNN classification, which often uses Hamming loss, but that is for prediction, not for identifying which *existing* cell is wrong.

3. **Per-finding embeddings** — train a representation that is specific to each finding. This is the expensive answer and I found no paper arguing it is necessary for multi-label label-error detection. The Cleanlab and SEMD results suggest good per-column probabilities from a jointly trained multi-label classifier are enough.

### The chest-X-ray paper you are looking for

**"Mislabel Detection in Multi-Label Chest X-Rays via Prototype-Weighted Neighborhood Consistency in CoAtNet Embedding Space" (Appl. Sci. 2026)** — [MDPI](https://www.mdpi.com/2076-3417/16/9/4067). I could not fetch the full PDF (MDPI blocked the request), but the abstract and results snippets say:
- It is training-free mislabel detection using neighbourhood consistency in a CoAtNet embedding space.
- It is evaluated on multi-label chest X-rays.
- It uses patient-disjoint splits and a top-20% review budget.
- It reports downstream macro-AUROC and Recall@budget under a 20% disjoint-label replacement regime.
- It compares raw frozen 2048-d CoAtNet features vs refined 64-d embeddings and finds the refined embeddings improve both method families.

This is the closest published work to "CheXpert cells rather than studies" that I found. If you need the exact per-cell definition they use, one of us will have to get past MDPI's challenge or email the authors.

### My read for our build

Use the shared study embedding and run **per-finding SimiFeat** (twelve independent kNN detectors), then pool the twelve per-cell scores into a study+cell ranking. The pooling matters: EMA over sorted scores is the only thing in the literature that has been measured to work better than a simple minimum for multi-label. Your current CL detector is already doing the per-column part; replacing CL's ranking with SimiFeat's neighbourhood-consistency ranking per column is the drop-in experiment.

