## Q-MISS. False-negative / missed-finding detection is harder, and the detector literature mostly looks the other way

### Is the false-negative half known to be harder?

Yes, in radiology specifically. **Noguchi et al., "Analysis of 'visible in retrospect' to monitor false-negative findings in radiological reports"** reports that false-negative errors account for **0.8–5% of daily clinical diagnostic imaging errors**, with false-negative rates of **13–90%** recorded depending on the study. A missed finding is structurally harder to detect because the evidence was not written down in the report and the model was not explicitly taught to expect it.

In our own numbers: misses read AUC 0.6903 and precision 0.08–0.11 at every budget, against AUC 0.7628 / precision 0.720 for all disagreements. That gap is consistent with the literature and not a bug in the detector.

### Is there a detector family aimed at unlabelled-positive ranking?

Not for label-error ranking, as far as I can find. **Positive-unlabelled (PU) learning** is the right conceptual framing — some positives are labelled, the rest are hidden in the unlabelled pool — but the literature uses it to **train classifiers**, not to rank existing negative labels as probable misses.

- **Bekker & Davis, "Learning from positive and unlabeled data: a survey" (ML 2020)** — the standard reference; covers PU classification, not label-error detection.
- **Zhao et al., "Positive-unlabeled learning for binary and multi-class cell detection in histopathology images with incomplete annotations" (MELBA 2023)** — directly uses PU learning for incomplete annotations in histopathology. The setting is identical in spirit: unannotated regions may contain positive cells. But the goal is training a better detector, not ranking which existing negative labels are wrong.
- **Jain et al., "Recovering True Classifier Performance in Positive-Unlabeled Learning" (AAAI 2017)** — performance estimation under PU assumptions, not ranking errors.

### What would actually attack the miss half

The CL / AUM / SimiFeat family is built on **confident disagreement with the given label**, which structurally favours false positives (a label says present, model says absent confidently). A missed finding is the opposite: label says absent, model has no strong signal because it was never taught the pattern.

Families that might rank misses better:
- **Anomaly / out-of-distribution detection** on the negative class. A study that looks like positives but is labelled negative everywhere is a candidate miss. This is not label-error detection literature, but it is the right mathematical shape.
- **PU learning with instance-level scoring**, e.g. estimating the probability that each "negative" cell is actually an unlabelled positive. Cleanlab has a `find_label_issues` mode for class-conditional noise, but I did not find a published comparison showing it beats CL on the miss half.
- **Contrastive learning with missing-label awareness** — embedding positives and unlabelleds such that unlabelled positives land near positives. This is training-centric, not a post-hoc detector.

### Bottom line

The miss half is the harder half and there is no off-the-shelf detector family I can point to that has been shown to rank missed findings well. If you want to attack it, the experiment is probably to treat every "absent" cell as unlabelled, every "present" cell as a labelled positive, and run a PU scorer to rank absent cells by estimated latent-positive probability. That would be a new build, not a literature drop-in.

---

So: Q-MULTI has a clear published answer (per-column detectors + EMA pooling), and Q-MISS is a real gap — the literature trains classifiers under incomplete annotations but does not rank existing negatives as missed errors.

